<title>USA: U.S. Jan import prices fall 0.3 pct.</title>
<headline>U.S. Jan import prices fall 0.3 pct.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-02-25</dateline>
<text>
<p>Labor Department report of U.S. import and export price changes by percent (unadjusted).</p>
<p>    -			    Jan    Dec-Rev from  Jan 96/95</p>
<p> ALL IMPORTS		  -0.3    0.1    0.1	0.8</p>
<p> -Petroleum		    1.3    0.2    0.3     27.0</p>
<p> -Nonpetroleum		-0.4    0.1    0.1     -1.9</p>
<p> -Food, Feed, Drink	 -0.3   -1.2   -1.2	1.3</p>
<p> -Industrial Supplies	1.2    0.4    0.3	8.2</p>
<p> -Capital Goods	     -1.8   -0.2   -0.3     -5.4</p>
<p> -Motor Vehicles,Parts    -0.1    0.2    0.3	0.1</p>
<p> -Consumer Goods ExAutos  -0.1   UNCH    0.1     -0.8</p>
<p>Percent changes, not seasonally adjusted --</p>
<p> ALL EXPORTS		   0.2   UNCH   UNCH     -1.5</p>
<p> -Agricultural		 0.1   -0.9   -0.9     -8.3</p>
<p> -Non-Agricultural	   0.1    0.1    0.1     -0.5</p>
<p> -Food, Feed, Drink	 -0.3   -1.2   -1.2     -8.7</p>
<p> -Industrial Supplies	0.2    0.2    0.4     -2.7</p>
<p> -Capital Goods		0.2    0.1   -0.1	0.1</p>
<p> -Motor Vehicles,Parts     0.2   UNCH   UNCH	0.7</p>
<p> -Consumer Goods ExAutos   0.1    0.2    0.2	1.2</p>
<p>The department reported January price indexes (1990 base year equals 100) of 101.4 for imports, and 99.5 for exports.</p>
<p>The 1.8 percent drop in January capital goods import prices, led by lower computer, peripheral and semiconductor costs, was its largest monthly drop since the department began reporting import prices on a monthly basis in 1989, it said.</p>
</text>
